2026-09-17 | scripts/manage_install.py | update() drops options when it falls back to a full install. On a machine with no .venv, `manage_install.py update --offline-bundle <dir>` called install(pytorch_runtime=...) WITHOUT the bundle, so an offline target would silently attempt networked installs despite being handed local wheels and models. | Forwarded offline_bundle in the fallback call (`return install(pytorch_runtime=..., offline_bundle=offline_bundle)`). Added regression test test_update_forwards_offline_bundle_to_full_install, which failed first with forwarded kwargs {'pytorch_runtime': 'auto'}. | tests/test_manage_install.py: 13 passed.

2026-09-17 | scripts/build_offline_bundle.py | The generated install.bat ran `--offline-bundle "%BUNDLE_DIR%"` where BUNDLE_DIR comes from %~dp0, which always ends in a backslash. The argument therefore expanded to `"C:\...\bundle\"`; PowerShell's argument parser reads the trailing backslash-quote as an escaped quote, mangling the path and breaking the Windows offline install. | Stripped the trailing backslash (`if "%BUNDLE_DIR:~-1%"=="\" set "BUNDLE_DIR=%BUNDLE_DIR:~0,-1%"`) and added the separator explicitly at the repo.tar.gz use site. Pinned the strip and the exact argument form in test_bundle_launchers_invoke_offline_install. | tests/test_offline_bundle.py: 15 passed.

