2026-09-17 | scripts/doctor.py | The launch gate was not idempotent: its verdict depended on how many times it had been run. Evidence, reproduced in a pristine tracked-tree copy (git archive HEAD, no .venv, no build/): run 1 printed "PASS Default voice sources: ... Seashells=2, fallback=0" and run 2 printed "fallback=2" on an unchanged machine, and that line was the ONLY difference between the two runs (byte-diff confirmed; runs 2 and 3 then agreed, i.e. it converged one run late). Root cause: the real-engine readiness check called ensure_real_engine_inputs(), which writes real_engine_dialogue.txt + speaker_a_ref.wav + speaker_b_ref.wav into build/real_engine_smoke/inputs — a directory voice_catalog_audit() counts among its build-time fallback voice sources. run() computes voice_sources before real_engine_smoke, so run 1 counted 0 and run 2 counted the clips run 1 had just written. A second defect in the same class as the one fixed last pass: diagnostic state created by the gate feeding the gate. | Made the check read-only — removed the ensure_real_engine_inputs() call (and its import) from _real_engine_smoke_status, with a comment stating why. Nothing is lost: real_engine_smoke.py calls ensure_real_engine_inputs() itself when the smoke is actually run (line 144), and real_engine_smoke_prerequisites() already reports dialogue/reference existence plus can_generate_missing_inputs. Added two regression tests: test_real_engine_readiness_does_not_write (keeps the REAL ensure_real_engine_inputs and asserts the check creates nothing) and test_doctor_output_is_identical_across_consecutive_runs (runs doctor.run() twice with the heavy probes stubbed, keeping the voice-source/real-engine coupling real, and asserts the printed report is byte-identical). Both FAILED against the unfixed code — the second with exactly "fallback=0" vs "fallback=2" — and pass after the fix. | Verified live: pristine tree, build/ deleted, three consecutive runs byte-identical with fallback=0 each and build/real_engine_smoke/inputs never created; this tree three consecutive runs identical; the full gate path (manage_install.py doctor twice, ./doctor_oracle_tts.sh twice) identical and the wrapper's output identical to the manager's; --json identical across runs once the inherent timing measurements (deterministic_smoke.runtime_seconds) are excluded. Suite: 892 passed / 0 failed / 0 errors (this tree); 891 passed / 1 skipped / 0 failed / 0 errors (pristine). Nothing pushed.

2026-09-17 | launch validation surface: audit verdicts (no change) | Audited every check in scripts/doctor.py, the doctor path of scripts/manage_install.py, and the wrapper scripts for the two failure modes (PASS on an artifact it did not produce/verify; output that is not idempotent). Judged LEGITIMATE AS-IS, with the verification that backs each: python / ffmpeg — live interpreter and PATH probes (presence IS the capability); entrypoint — actually executes `the-oracle --help` both directly and in a fresh login shell, not merely a file-exists test; chatterbox_import, chatterbox_init, perth — real import and real model construction (init is skipped only when --skip-model-init is passed); turbo — turbo_readiness_report() loads ChatterboxTurboTTS.from_local() and sets ok only after the load succeeds, so its PASS is not a directory-existence test; qt — creates an offscreen QApplication rather than only checking a plugin path; deterministic_smoke — runs a render and verifies the output it produced exists and is non-empty; cuda_backend / vulkan_backend — live nvidia-smi/vulkaninfo probes, and the audio.cpp CLI is executed to enumerate devices; voice_sources — its PASS is legitimate (bundled tracked clips in Seashells/generic satisfy ok on a truly fresh tree); only its fallback COUNT was unstable, fixed by the entry above. Scope limits respected: no new checks or features added, the legitimate guard skips were left alone (tests/test_vulkan_backend.py still skips when the gitignored audio.cpp build is absent — no native dependencies were built), and bootstrap/install were not run for real. One residual, judged legitimate and left as-is: the --json report's timing measurements (deterministic_smoke.runtime_seconds, and chatterbox_init.seconds when init runs) necessarily differ between runs; they are measurements of the machine, not state, and the human report does not print them. Also noted, not a defect: this working tree still carries build/real_engine_smoke/inputs written